WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.391
A workflow can start from a web request, answer it, and call other web services.

00:00:06.391 --> 00:00:11.217
The HTTP Workflows Activities feature provides the activities for that.

00:00:11.217 --> 00:00:15.417
In this video, a webhook checks a product in a stock API, and

00:00:15.417 --> 00:00:18.300
we cover signals, filters and redirects.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.604
The HTTP Workflows Activities feature connects a workflow to the web.

00:00:27.604 --> 00:00:30.238
It depends on the Workflows feature.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:35.067
An HTTP Request event starts a workflow when a request reaches its URL,

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:40.900
and Filter Incoming HTTP Request starts one when a request reaches a controller action.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:46.730
HTTP Response and HTTP Redirect answer the caller of the workflow.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.116
The HTTP Request task works the other way:

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:54.490
it calls a web API, and the workflow reads the response.

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:03.976
Open a workflow in the designer, and search the activities for HTTP.

00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:07.161
The HTTP category lists six activities.

00:01:07.161 --> 00:01:09.496
HTTP Request appears twice:

00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:12.539
once as an event, and once as a task.

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:18.783
Select Events to see the three that start a workflow or wait for something:

00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:23.135
HTTP Request, Filter Incoming HTTP Request, and Signal.

00:01:23.611 --> 00:01:27.764
Select Tasks for the three that do something:

00:01:27.764 --> 00:01:32.081
HTTP Redirect, HTTP Request, and HTTP Response.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.814
This workflow checks the stock of a product.

00:01:39.814 --> 00:01:44.925
An HTTP Request event starts it, a Script reads the request, an HTTP

00:01:44.925 --> 00:01:50.520
Request task calls a stock API, and an HTTP Response replies to the caller.

00:01:51.003 --> 00:01:53.254
Open the HTTP Request event.

00:01:53.254 --> 00:01:58.617
HTTP Method is POST, and the URL is generated for this activity; Regenerate

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.603
creates a different one.

00:02:00.603 --> 00:02:05.503
Anyone who calls this URL triggers the workflow, so keep it private.

00:02:05.975 --> 00:02:09.562
Validate Antiforgery Token is checked by default.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:13.997
Uncheck it when a service calls the URL as a webhook, as here.

00:02:13.997 --> 00:02:19.345
Token Life Span is a number of days, and zero means the token never expires.

00:02:23.545 --> 00:02:26.165
A Script activity reads the request.

00:02:26.165 --> 00:02:30.532
readBody returns the raw body, and the script parses it as JSON.

00:02:30.532 --> 00:02:37.705
setProperty keeps the product code for the activities that follow, and setOutcome lets the workflow continue.

00:02:38.455 --> 00:02:41.827
Other functions read other parts of a request.

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:46.366
queryString and requestForm return the value of a field by name.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:52.201
deserializeRequestData returns the query string, the posted form or the JSON body as

00:02:52.201 --> 00:02:56.675
one object, and httpContext returns the context of the request.

00:02:57.425 --> 00:03:00.342
A script can also answer the request:

00:03:00.342 --> 00:03:03.598
responseWrite writes text to the response.

00:03:03.598 --> 00:03:09.092
When no activity writes a response, such as HTTP Response, HTTP Redirect or

00:03:09.092 --> 00:03:14.925
responseWrite, the caller of the HTTP Request event's URL receives 202 Accepted.

00:03:19.225 --> 00:03:21.457
Open the HTTP Request task.

00:03:21.457 --> 00:03:27.205
Its URL, Headers and Body accept Liquid, so the product code goes into the URL.

00:03:27.205 --> 00:03:32.885
Content Type applies to the body, which is sent only with POST, PUT and PATCH.

00:03:38.012 --> 00:03:43.144
HTTP Response Codes lists the status codes the workflow handles.

00:03:43.144 --> 00:03:47.983
Each code becomes an outcome, 200 OK and 404 Not Found here.

00:03:47.983 --> 00:03:52.602
Any other status takes the Unhandled Http Status outcome.

00:03:53.122 --> 00:03:57.954
The Outputs tab stores the response body, the status code or the whole

00:03:57.954 --> 00:03:59.798
response in a variable.

00:03:59.798 --> 00:04:03.168
Here, the response body is stored in stockBody.

00:04:03.168 --> 00:04:10.542
The last result also holds the response, with Body, Headers, StatusCode, ReasonPhrase and IsSuccessStatusCode.

00:04:14.335 --> 00:04:18.088
An HTTP Response activity replies to the caller.

00:04:18.088 --> 00:04:22.953
It has an HTTP Status Code, a Content Type, Content and Headers.

00:04:22.953 --> 00:04:27.610
The 200 branch returns the body of the stock API, and the 404

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.765
branch returns a message.

00:04:34.065 --> 00:04:39.837
Run… in the toolbar sends a request to the published workflow, as a client would.

00:04:39.837 --> 00:04:43.486
The event is a POST, so the dialog offers a body.

00:04:43.486 --> 00:04:46.405
Enter a JSON body with a product code.

00:04:50.136 --> 00:04:51.207
Select Run.

00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:55.552
The response is 200, with the body that the stock API returned.

00:04:55.552 --> 00:05:01.346
Below it, the dialog shows the instance this request started, and a button to open it.

00:05:02.713 --> 00:05:08.257
Change the product code to one the stock API does not know, and run it again.

00:05:08.257 --> 00:05:15.003
The API answers 404, the workflow follows the 404 outcome, and the caller receives the message.

00:05:19.203 --> 00:05:22.016
A Signal event makes a workflow wait.

00:05:22.016 --> 00:05:25.613
It resumes when someone opens a link made for it.

00:05:25.613 --> 00:05:31.303
In Liquid, the signal_url filter makes the link, and in a script, signalUrl does.

00:05:32.053 --> 00:05:35.371
The link has a protected token, valid for seven days.

00:05:35.371 --> 00:05:39.532
It is a path on the site, so add the site address before sending it.

00:05:39.532 --> 00:05:43.581
Opening it resumes the workflow that made the link, if it waits on

00:05:43.581 --> 00:05:45.493
a Signal with the same name.

00:05:49.693 --> 00:05:55.681
Filter Incoming HTTP Request starts a workflow when a request reaches a controller action.

00:05:55.681 --> 00:06:00.422
You give it the HTTP Method, Controller, Action and Area to match, and

00:06:00.422 --> 00:06:03.353
the workflow runs before the action does.

00:06:04.103 --> 00:06:09.218
HTTP Redirect sends the caller to a Location, which accepts Liquid.

00:06:09.218 --> 00:06:14.473
Check Permanent for a 301 redirect, and leave it unchecked for a 302.

00:06:15.223 --> 00:06:20.662
To move a workflow to another site, export it as a recipe and import it there.

00:06:20.662 --> 00:06:26.553
The import generates the URL of each HTTP Request event on the site that receives it.

00:06:28.553 --> 00:06:34.223
That is how a workflow receives a request, calls a web API, and answers the caller.

00:06:34.223 --> 00:06:39.257
Signals, filters and redirects extend it to links and controller actions.

00:06:39.257 --> 00:06:40.913
Thanks for watching.

